## Where JavaScript Lives

- **Entry** — `app/javascript/application.js` imports Turbo, registers Stimulus, and pulls in any pinned modules.
- **Stimulus** — `app/javascript/controllers/<name>_controller.js`, kebab-case filenames matching `data-controller="<name>"`.
- **Shared modules** — `app/javascript/lib/<thing>.js` (pinned or bundled), imported by controllers. Use **ES modules** with named exports; avoid default exports for shared code so call sites read clearly (Stimulus controllers are the exception — Stimulus expects the controller class as the default export). One concern per file — a `lib/` module exporting six unrelated helpers gets split.
- **Layouts** — `javascript_importmap_tags` (importmap) or the helper your bundler documents — not ad hoc `<script src="https://cdn…">` for app logic. With Content Security Policy enabled, the importmap and Stimulus pipelines respect Rails' nonce helpers — don't hand-roll `<script>` tags that bypass them.

## Boundary Rules

- **HTML + Turbo** own navigation, persistence, and authorization for first-party pages.
- **Stimulus** enhances the DOM; it does not replace routes, sessions, or authorization.
- **No parallel client router** or JSON-first CRUD for pages you already render with ERB.
- **No client-only source of truth** for domain state. The server's HTML is the contract.
- **Hiding UI in JS is UX, not security** — anything hidden client-side is still callable via direct request; authorization runs on the server.

## Modern JavaScript Hygiene

Default to the modern ECMAScript subset Rails and Hotwire already assume:

- **`const` by default**, `let` when reassignment is genuine, **never `var`**. No top-level mutation of imported modules.
- **ES modules** (`import` / `export`) — no AMD, no CommonJS, no `window.MyApp = …` globals.
- **Arrow functions** for callbacks; **named functions** when stack traces matter (event handlers, debouncers).
- **`async` / `await`** over raw `.then` chains. Wrap awaits in `try / catch` only where you can do something useful — let unexpected failures bubble.
- **Strict equality** (`===`, `!==`); no truthiness comparisons for IDs or numeric fields.
- **Template literals** over string concatenation. **Destructuring** for parameters and return shapes.
- **Optional chaining** (`?.`) and **nullish coalescing** (`??`) instead of long `&&` chains.
- **`for…of`** over `forEach` when you need `await` inside the loop (`forEach` does **not** await — async callbacks in it are a bug); **`map` / `filter` / `reduce`** for pure transforms.

Format with the team's existing tools (Prettier or the bundler default). Lint with **ESLint** when bundling; with importmap, lean on code review and the conventions in this rule.

## Stimulus Controllers — Anatomy

Stimulus controllers are **small, single-purpose, and DOM-driven**. They derive state from `targets`, `values`, `classes`, and the DOM itself — not from internal closures that get stranded by Turbo morphing.

- **One responsibility per controller**, named in the filename (`clipboard_controller.js`, not `ui_controller.js`). A controller that toggles a panel **and** debounces a search **and** posts a fetch is three controllers.
- **Under ~100 lines**, reading top-to-bottom. Past that, split — `agent_harness_rails/skills/refactoring-stimulus-controllers/SKILL.md`.
- **`static targets / values / classes / outlets`** — not ad hoc `querySelector` calls or hard-coded class strings.
- **`connect()` / `disconnect()`** for setup and teardown of *external* state only (event listeners on `window`/`document`, timers, observers). Same-element listeners are wired via `data-action` — don't `addEventListener` what HTML can express.
- **Always clean up in `disconnect()`** — Turbo navigations re-run lifecycle hooks, and leaked listeners pile up.
- **CustomEvents** (`this.dispatch("opened")`) for cross-controller signals without a hard dependency; **outlets when the dependency is structural**. No global event buses.
- **Values change callbacks** (`xValueChanged`) react to attribute mutations — useful for morph-friendly state, since the DOM (not a JS variable) is the source of truth.

Worked example, lifecycle callbacks, and split rules: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-javascript/references/patterns.md`** § Stimulus Controllers — Anatomy; data-attribute syntax in ERB — **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-hotwire/references/patterns.md`** § Stimulus.

## Fetching from JavaScript

Reach for `fetch` only when HTML + Turbo cannot express the interaction (e.g. presigned upload to S3, third-party widget). When you do:

- Use **`@rails/request.js`** (omakase) or hand-build `fetch` with the **`X-CSRF-Token`** header. Never bypass CSRF for first-party endpoints.
- Send **`Accept: text/vnd.turbo-stream.html`** when the response is a Turbo Stream; let Turbo handle the DOM update.
- Wrap network calls in `try / catch`; render an error message in the DOM, never `alert()` or swallow silently.
- **Never embed secrets or API keys** in client-side JS. Server-side proxies, signed URLs, or server-rendered tokens only.

## Accessibility and DOM Safety

- Toggle **`aria-*`** and **`hidden`** attributes, not just CSS classes, when state changes (expanded, selected, busy).
- Manage **focus** explicitly on overlay/modal open and close; restore focus on close, and trap it inside true modal dialogs.
- Honor keyboard interaction: `Escape` closes overlays, `Enter` / `Space` activates buttons, `Tab` moves focus normally.
- Use **`textContent`** (or template rendering via Turbo) instead of `innerHTML` for user-controlled strings. If `innerHTML` is genuinely needed, the string must come from a server-rendered response.
- Respect **`prefers-reduced-motion`** when adding animation (`window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches`).
- Never call `eval`, `new Function`, or assign to `window.location` with user input; don't assemble URLs from user input without `encodeURIComponent`.

## Performance

- Debounce/throttle high-frequency input handlers (search-as-you-type, resize). A 150–300 ms debounce is the usual default; `requestAnimationFrame` is the usual ceiling for scroll/resize throttling.
- Use `{ passive: true }` for scroll/touch listeners that don't `preventDefault`.
- Tear down `MutationObserver` / `IntersectionObserver` in `disconnect()`.
- Defer non-critical scripts with the layout's standard `defer` (`javascript_importmap_tags` already does this) — don't ship `async` scripts that race Turbo.
- Importmap apps: **pin the minified build** of vendor modules; do not import dev builds into production.

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Instead |
|--------------|---------|
| Two competing JS pipelines (importmap + bundler both loading Turbo) | One strategy; simplify |
| `fetch` + JSON for every mutation on same-origin pages | `form_with` + Turbo Streams / Frames |
| Client-only source of truth for domain state | Server session + HTML |
| God-controller doing five unrelated things | Several focused controllers composed via outlets / events |
| `connect()` adding listeners that `disconnect()` does not remove | Always pair `addEventListener` with `removeEventListener` |
| Closures holding state that morphing strands | Read state from `data-*-value` / DOM each time |
| `querySelector` reaching into the DOM past `static targets` | Declare targets — selectors and magic strings defeat Stimulus' contract |
| Hard-coded class strings (`classList.add("bg-red-500")`) in controllers | `static classes`, defined in the ERB |
| `innerHTML = userInput` | `textContent`, or a server-rendered Turbo response |
| `alert()` / `console.log` shipped to production | Inline DOM error messages; structured logging behind a debug flag |
| `window.MyApp.something` globals | ES module exports; Stimulus outlets/events for cross-controller wiring |
| Global event buses (`window.dispatchEvent` to coordinate controllers) | `this.dispatch` + outlets |
| `node_modules` added per feature with no build requirement | Pin via importmap; bundling is an intentional decision |
| Secrets in JS bundles | Server-side proxy or signed URL |
| Micro-frontends / module federation for a monolith-shaped Rails app | One pipeline; opposite of omakase |

## Verification

Each Stimulus behaviour earns at most **one canonical system spec**, on the simplest page that exercises it — other usages assume it works; budget: `agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-tests/references/system-specs.md` § Budget. For an existing fleet of controllers without specs, run `agent_harness_rails/skills/refactoring-stimulus-controllers/SKILL.md`.

- [ ] One primary JS delivery path (importmap **or** documented bundler — not two masters)
- [ ] Stimulus lives under `app/javascript/controllers/` and is registered from the app entrypoint
- [ ] Layout uses `javascript_importmap_tags` or the bundler's documented tag — not ad hoc `<script src>` for app code
- [ ] No client-side router duplicating Rails routes for same-origin pages
- [ ] No JSON API exists only to feed your own ERB pages
- [ ] Adding npm packages is justified — not "npm install" by default for CRUD
- [ ] Each Stimulus controller has a single responsibility and is under ~100 lines
- [ ] Stimulus controllers use `static targets / values / classes / outlets`, not query selectors and magic strings
- [ ] `connect()` is paired with `disconnect()` for any external listeners, timers, or observers
- [ ] State is derived from the DOM — controllers survive a Turbo morph
- [ ] `fetch` calls go through `@rails/request.js` (or include CSRF token) and have `try / catch` error handling
- [ ] No secrets, API keys, or auth tokens are embedded in client JS
- [ ] No `innerHTML` with user-controlled input; user input goes through `textContent` or server-rendered HTML
- [ ] Overlay/modal Stimulus controllers manage focus and toggle `aria-*` attributes
- [ ] Each Stimulus behaviour has at most one canonical system spec on the simplest page that exercises it
